sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/wolfram2002-ch04-10> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>;
    dc:description "Sequences defined by simple properties of numbers (primes, divisor sums, representations as sums of squares or primes) show complexity that persists despite centuries of number theory, and this complexity arises as a fundamental consequence of the rules generating them, just as in other simple programs.";
    dc:type "general-principle";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Complexity in number-theoretic sequences arises from their generating rules";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "Stephen Wolfram, A New Kind of Science (Champaign, IL: Wolfram Media, 2002), Chapter 4, “Systems Based on Numbers”; section “The Sequence of Primes” (from p. 132), p. 134. Scope: integer sequences defined by simple number-theoretic properties"
}
